f go through this list  there are many kind of scenarios where  we see similarity but one overriding  theme that i observed when looking at  this is that many of these studies show  treatments with some kind of uh compound  that induces dna damage so kind of makes  sense and again most of these are  cancer-related studies  now the last step here would be to  select any number of studies that i deem  relevant i can just select them within  the table and now open up this gene heap  app to ask the question  we see conservation of  this loss-of-function pattern in our  data set with regards to gene expression  in public studies now what specific  genes  whose loss of function are identified in  my data set in network one in the first  column in this table actually show  decreased expression in any of these  studies  and what will generate is this heat  map-based view where as i scroll down  i'll start to see a number of  genes within a cluster here  that would say that they show mlh1 again  a lot of the fancy family of genes  praca those all have loss of function  variants in my lint syndrome cohort  and very interestingly across these  studies again different cancers treated  with dna damaging agents  we see that these genes in fact show a  decrease in expressions of the green  cells  across each of these different columns  so now i have some insights and i can  explore this further  when we talk about public comparisons we  have lots of different comparisons  available in the context of as we're  looking at here many treatment-based  studies but also disease versus normal  responders versus non-responders but  hopefully this represents a way in which  we can integrate loss of function  information  from a variant  based study to gene expression patterns  within public data sets  so with that uh i'd just like to  conclude i'd like to mention again  everything that we've looked at today  really falls under our kaiogen discovery  insights portfolio  we talked about earlier using the  kaiogen clc genomics workbench to move  from fast q to identification of  variance and then for interpretation  purposes we talked about at the variant  metal level kaijin clinical insights  interpret translational and ingenuity  pathway analysis  and ultimately hopefully what i've shown  everyone today is that we've walked  through this step of going from fast q  files  using clc genomics workbench to analyze  and call variants that are accurate and  with confidence  from there using hygiene clinical  insights interpret translational to  prioritize those variants both within  samples and across a cohort that are  pathogenic and in this case represent a  loss of function of a given gene set of  interest and then lastly utilize  ingenuity pathway analysis for discovery  taking that gene set with the loss of  function variance in determining what  pathways are relevant  how does this loss-of-function signature  relate to gene expression signatures in  various public cancer studies  so with that i'd like to thank everybody  uh for listening in today and at this  point i will open it up to any questions  that you may have  great thank you so much eric that was a  great presentation thanks for walking us  through all those steps and putting  those pieces together again last chance  to ask some questions here today we've  got a few questions here in the queue  uh let's start off with this first one  um  can we use it to check somatic  variations for tumor samples without the  new without the normal reference i have  ngs sequence data available for only 40  tumor samples with no normal data  yes so in the genomics workbench we have  pre-built pipelines for workflows that  can run in either of two contexts for  somatic data so to your scenario if you  let's say are missing a match normal you  can call variants um and perhaps you  could employ a filtering step where you  look at those variant alleles that are  in db snip you can filter them out to  perhaps remo